<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Company of One Hundred Associates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1627–1663</span> · Also known as Compagnie des Cent-Associés</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Company of One Hundred Associates was a French trading company created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> in 1627 and given ownership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> and a monopoly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, in return for bringing out settlers. The capture of its first fleet and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> by the Kirke brothers in 1628–1629 crippled it, and it settled few colonists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> dissolved it in 1663 and made New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> a royal province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/company-of-one-hundred-associates.jpg" alt="Company of One Hundred Associates" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Standard_of_King_Louis_XIV.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
